::GEO_U02_L01_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which word names the original figure before a transformation? {
= Preimage#Correct. The preimage is the figure before the transformation.
~ Image#The image is the figure after the transformation.
~ Vertex#A vertex is a point where sides or rays meet, not the original figure.
~ Coordinate rule#A coordinate rule describes how points move.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>If A maps to A', what does A' represent? {
~ The original point#The original point is A. The prime mark names the image point.
= The image of point A#Correct. A' is read A prime and names the image of A.
~ A point unrelated to A#Prime notation shows a relationship to the original point.
~ The midpoint of A#A' does not indicate a midpoint.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule translates a point 5 units left and 1 unit up? {
~ (x, y) → (x + 5, y - 1)#This moves right and down, the opposite directions.
~ (x, y) → (x - 1, y + 5)#This switches the horizontal and vertical changes.
= (x, y) → (x - 5, y + 1)#Correct. Left decreases x by 5, and up increases y by 1.
~ (x, y) → (-x, y)#This is a reflection across the y-axis, not a translation.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Point P(-2, 3) is translated by (x, y) → (x + 4, y - 6). What is P'? {
~ (-6, 9)#This subtracts 4 from x and adds 6 to y, reversing the rule.
~ (2, 9)#The x-coordinate is correct, but the y-coordinate should decrease.
~ (-6, -3)#The y-coordinate is correct, but the x-coordinate should increase.
= (2, -3)#Correct. -2 + 4 = 2 and 3 - 6 = -3.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule represents reflection across the x-axis? {
= (x, y) → (x, -y)#Correct. Reflection across the x-axis keeps x and changes the sign of y.
~ (x, y) → (-x, y)#This is reflection across the y-axis.
~ (x, y) → (-x, -y)#This changes both signs.
~ (x, y) → (y, x)#This switches coordinates.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Point D(6, -2) is reflected across the y-axis. What is D'? {
~ (6, 2)#This changes y, which matches reflection across the x-axis.
= (-6, -2)#Correct. Reflection across the y-axis changes the sign of x only.
~ (-6, 2)#This changes both coordinate signs.
~ (-2, 6)#This switches the coordinates instead of reflecting.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule represents reflection through the origin? {
~ (x, y) → (x, -y)#This reflects across the x-axis only.
~ (x, y) → (-x, y)#This reflects across the y-axis only.
= (x, y) → (-x, -y)#Correct. Reflection through the origin changes both signs.
~ (x, y) → (x + 1, y + 1)#This is a translation, not a reflection.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Point E(-3, -4) is reflected through the origin. What is E'? {
~ (-3, 4)#This changes y only.
~ (3, -4)#This changes x only.
~ (-4, -3)#This switches coordinates.
= (3, 4)#Correct. Reflection through the origin changes both coordinate signs.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule represents a 90° counterclockwise rotation about the origin? {
= (x, y) → (-y, x)#Correct. This is the standard 90° counterclockwise rotation rule.
~ (x, y) → (y, -x)#This is the 90° clockwise rotation rule.
~ (x, y) → (-x, -y)#This is a 180° rotation rule.
~ (x, y) → (x, -y)#This is reflection across the x-axis.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Point F(5, -1) is rotated 90° counterclockwise about the origin. What is F'? {
~ (-1, -5)#This is the 90° clockwise rotation result.
= (1, 5)#Correct. Use (x, y) → (-y, x), so (5, -1) maps to (1, 5).
~ (-5, 1)#This is a reflection through the origin.
~ (5, 1)#This is reflection across the x-axis.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule represents a 90° clockwise rotation about the origin? {
~ (x, y) → (-y, x)#This is the 90° counterclockwise rotation rule.
~ (x, y) → (-x, -y)#This is the 180° rotation rule.
= (x, y) → (y, -x)#Correct. This is the standard 90° clockwise rotation rule.
~ (x, y) → (-x, y)#This is reflection across the y-axis.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Point G(-2, 6) is rotated 90° clockwise about the origin. What is G'? {
~ (-6, -2)#This is the 90° counterclockwise result.
~ (2, -6)#This is a 180° rotation result.
~ (-2, -6)#This reflects across the x-axis.
= (6, 2)#Correct. Use (x, y) → (y, -x), so (-2, 6) maps to (6, 2).
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule represents a 180° rotation about the origin? {
= (x, y) → (-x, -y)#Correct. A 180° rotation changes both coordinate signs.
~ (x, y) → (x, -y)#This reflects across the x-axis.
~ (x, y) → (-x, y)#This reflects across the y-axis.
~ (x, y) → (-y, x)#This is a 90° counterclockwise rotation.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Point H(7, -3) is rotated 180° about the origin. What is H'? {
~ (7, 3)#This changes y only.
= (-7, 3)#Correct. A 180° rotation changes both signs.
~ (-7, -3)#This changes x only.
~ (3, -7)#This switches coordinates.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Point J(2, 4) maps to J'(6, 1). Which translation describes the movement? {
~ 4 units left and 3 units up#This reverses both coordinate changes.
~ 3 units right and 4 units down#This switches the coordinate changes.
= 4 units right and 3 units down#Correct. x increases by 4, and y decreases by 3.
~ 6 units right and 1 unit up#This uses the image coordinates instead of the changes.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Point K(-5, 2) maps to K'(-5, -2). Which transformation is shown? {
~ Reflection across the y-axis#Reflection across the y-axis would change x, not y.
~ Translation 5 units down#Only y changes by -4, not -5, and this pattern also matches a reflection.
~ Rotation 180°#A 180° rotation would change both signs.
= Reflection across the x-axis#Correct. x stays the same while y changes sign.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Point L(-8, 1) maps to L'(8, 1). Which transformation is shown? {
= Reflection across the y-axis#Correct. y stays the same while x changes sign.
~ Reflection across the x-axis#Reflection across the x-axis would change y.
~ Rotation 90° clockwise#A 90° clockwise rotation would switch coordinates.
~ Translation 8 units right#The x-coordinate changes by 16, not 8.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation maps (x, y) to (x - 2, y + 7)? {
~ Reflection across the x-axis#A reflection changes signs, not adds fixed values.
= Translation 2 units left and 7 units up#Correct. x decreases by 2, and y increases by 7.
~ Translation 7 units left and 2 units up#This switches the horizontal and vertical changes.
~ Rotation 180°#A 180° rotation changes both signs.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation maps every point (x, y) to (-x, y)? {
~ Reflection across the x-axis#Reflection across the x-axis changes y.
~ Rotation 180°#A 180° rotation changes both signs.
= Reflection across the y-axis#Correct. Reflection across the y-axis changes x and keeps y.
~ Translation left#A translation changes coordinates by fixed amounts, not signs.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation maps every point (x, y) to (y, -x)? {
~ 90° counterclockwise rotation#The 90° counterclockwise rule is (-y, x).
~ Reflection across the y-axis#Reflection across the y-axis is (-x, y).
~ Translation right and down#A translation adds or subtracts constants.
= 90° clockwise rotation#Correct. The rule (x, y) → (y, -x) is 90° clockwise.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation maps every point (x, y) to (-y, x)? {
= 90° counterclockwise rotation#Correct. This is the 90° counterclockwise rotation rule.
~ 90° clockwise rotation#The 90° clockwise rule is (y, -x).
~ Reflection across the x-axis#Reflection across the x-axis is (x, -y).
~ Translation up#A translation changes coordinates by fixed amounts.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A point moves from (-1, -2) to (-1, 5). Which description is correct? {
~ Translate 6 units up#The y-coordinate increases by 7, not 6.
= Translate 7 units up#Correct. x stays the same and y increases from -2 to 5.
~ Reflect across the x-axis#Reflection across the x-axis would map (-1, -2) to (-1, 2).
~ Rotate 180°#A 180° rotation would map (-1, -2) to (1, 2).
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A point moves from (3, 4) to (-3, -4). Which transformation could describe the movement? {
~ Reflection across the x-axis#This would map (3, 4) to (3, -4).
~ Reflection across the y-axis#This would map (3, 4) to (-3, 4).
= Rotation 180° about the origin#Correct. A 180° rotation changes both coordinate signs.
~ Translation 3 units left#A translation 3 units left would map the point to (0, 4).
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly describes (x, y) → (x + 6, y)? {
~ Move 6 units left#Left would be x - 6.
~ Move 6 units up#Up would change the y-coordinate.
~ Reflect across the y-axis#Reflection across the y-axis changes x to -x.
= Move 6 units right#Correct. Adding 6 to x moves every point 6 units right.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which coordinate rule shows no change to a point? {
= (x, y) → (x, y)#Correct. The image coordinates are identical to the preimage coordinates.
~ (x, y) → (-x, y)#This reflects across the y-axis.
~ (x, y) → (x + 1, y)#This translates right 1 unit.
~ (x, y) → (y, -x)#This rotates 90° clockwise.
}
